#%PAM-1.0
# PAM stack for BOTH roles of the wmde-greeter binary:
#  - the greeter authenticating a user at login (named in data/wmde-greeter.toml),
#  - the locker re-authenticating the owner of a locked session (src/locker.rs).
#
# Upstream ships a Debian stack (@include common-auth) that has no meaning here, and
# the Arch cosmic-greeter package sidesteps the question by pointing greetd at the
# `login` service - which leaves the LOCKER naming a service that does not exist, so
# PAM falls through to `other` and no password is ever accepted. Hence our own file.
#
# system-login, NOT system-auth. Only system-login carries `-session optional
# pam_systemd.so`, and that is what registers the session with logind and exports
# XDG_RUNTIME_DIR. system-auth has pam_systemd_home.so, which is homed and a different
# module entirely. Without pam_systemd the greeter session comes up with no runtime
# directory, wmde-comp panics with RuntimeDirNotSet before it draws anything, greetd
# reports "greeter exited without creating a session", and the screen stays black. The
# same applies to the session of whoever logs in through the greeter. Arch's own
# /etc/pam.d/greetd includes system-local-login for exactly this reason.
auth       include   system-login
account    include   system-login
password   include   system-login
session    include   system-login
